  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        518.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "In July 1909, BASF envoys gathered round a squat machine on a bench in Fritz Haber's Karlsruhe laboratory. Behind its gleaming, bolted valves, nitrogen and hydrogen met at crushing pressure and furnace heat, and transparent beads of ammonia began to drip slowly. Haber had pried nourishment from the air."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        410.9614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "BASF's Carl Bosch scaled the method up, and from 1913 a gigantic plant at Oppau mass-produced ammonia. Hailed for making bread from air, Haber won the coveted 1918 Nobel Prize. Ploughed into stony soils as fertiliser, ammonia wards off famine as no manure heap could, bringing abundance. By 2008 it was feeding nearly half of mankind."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        303.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        402.4614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Haber's ambition had a sinister side. Ammonia stocked Germany's arsenal with nitrates for munitions despite a British blockade, and Captain Haber marshalled chemical warfare. On 22 April 1915 at Ypres, troops vented about 168 tonnes of greenish, poisonous chlorine towards the Allied trenches, the first large-scale gas onslaught."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        215.7614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        294.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Haber's wife, Clara Immerwahr, Germany's first woman with a doctoral degree in chemistry, voiced objections to his work. On 2 May 1915, after festivities in his honour, she shot herself in the garden with his service pistol. The next day the widower left for the Eastern Front."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        128.0614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        207.2614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Prestige offered no sanctuary. Of Jewish origin, Haber left Germany an outcast after Hitler's regime seized power, dying in Basel in 1934. His institute's pesticide research had produced Zyklon. A later variant, Zyklon B, was used in Nazi death camps, where relatives of his died."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        79.9614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        119.5614173228347
      ],
      "text": "About half the nitrogen in our proteins and DNA has passed through a Haber-Bosch factory, so each of us carries a little of Haber's chemistry."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        517.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1909年7月，BASF的代表们聚集在一台矮胖的机器周围，机器就放在弗里茨·哈伯位于卡尔斯鲁厄的实验室里的一张工作台上。在它闪闪发亮、用螺栓紧固的阀门后面，氮和氢在压碎般的压力和熔炉般的高温下相遇，透明的氨（凝成的）液体小滴开始缓缓地滴落。哈伯从空气中撬出了养分。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        409.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "BASF的卡尔·博施扩大了该方法的规模，自1913年起，奥保的一座庞大的工厂大批量生产氨。因“从空气中造面包”而广受赞誉，哈伯赢得了人人梦寐以求的令人垂涎的1918年诺贝尔奖。被翻耕进多石的土壤，作为化肥，氨抵御着饥荒，这是任何粪肥垒成的肥堆都做不到的，并带来丰饶。到2008年，它养活了将近一半的人类。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        320.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        400.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "哈伯的雄心也有险恶的/邪恶的一面。氨用（制造）弹药所需的硝酸盐充实了德国的军火库，尽管有英国封锁，而哈伯上尉则组织调度了化学战。1915年4月22日，在伊普尔，（德国）部队朝协约国战壕方向排放了约168吨黄绿色、有毒的氯气——首次大规模毒气猛攻。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        252.16141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        312.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "哈伯的妻子克拉拉·伊梅瓦尔，是德国第一位获得化学博士学位的女性，她曾对他的工作表达了反对。1915年5月2日，在为他致敬的庆祝活动之后，她在花园里用他的军用手枪自尽了。第二天，这位丧妻之人动身前往东线。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        163.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        243.66141732283472
      ],
      "text": "声望没有提供（给他）庇护。身为犹太裔，哈伯在希特勒政权夺取了权力之后，作为一个被驱逐的人离开了德国，并于1934年死于巴塞尔。他研究所的杀虫剂研究曾催生了齐克隆。后来的变体齐克隆B被用于纳粹死亡营，他的一些亲属就死在那里。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        115.16141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        155.16141732283472
      ],
      "text": "我们蛋白质和DNA中约有一半的氮，都经过了哈伯-博施工厂；因此，我们每个人体内都携带着一点哈伯的化学。"
    }
  ],
